WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:07.200
Dragons cast a large shadow over the mythologies
of the world.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.299
Few legendary monsters can compare to these
gigantic beasts.

00:00:12.299 --> 00:00:16.510
But what if dragons cast… a slightly smaller
shadow?

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:22.000
What if some dragons weren’t huge monsters,
but strange and secretive creatures evolved

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.450
to live hidden lives in the shadows and margins
of the world?

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:32.660
The amazing artist and writer Andy Frazer
imagines a world where dragons are all around

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.710
us, from the highest mountains to the deepest
forest.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:43.469
Concentrated primarily in the author’s homeland
of Wales, these smaller dragons — or ‘Microdragons’

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:48.770
as I call them – make up for their reduced
size with their incredible diversity and ingenious

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:50.719
survival strategies.

00:00:50.719 --> 00:00:56.090
The author has released many field guides
on these tiny wonders that explore their evolution,

00:00:56.090 --> 00:01:02.289
anatomy, behavior, and habitats in great detail
— which you can find linked below.

00:01:02.289 --> 00:01:07.240
So, for this entry into the archive, I’ll
assume the role of a naturalist and observe

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.820
some of these creatures out in the field.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:19.780
Now, let’s set off into the Welsh countryside,
and discover the secret biology of microdragons…

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:24.450
At the start of the expedition, the first
place I look for microdragons is on the low

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.990
branches of trees.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:31.119
Here, a Draco Notophthalmus suns themselves
in the morning light.

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:36.040
While this stunning specimen can fly like
a bird, internally, they have several key

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.360
distinctions that make their anatomy unique.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.829
If we examined their skeleton, we’d find
dragon bones are denser than a bird’s, making

00:01:44.829 --> 00:01:49.580
them better suited to life on the ground,
where most dragon species spend part of their

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:50.700
time.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:56.450
In many species the neural spines along the
backbone are also greatly enlarged, forming

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.469
obvious spikes.

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:03.820
In the case of Draco Nothophthalmus, these
long spikes can be raised and lowered as a

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.250
threat display.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:10.360
For the Nothophthalmus survives by trying
to appear as dangerous and intimidating as

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:16.410
possible — making loud vocalizations and
threatening postures as I pass by.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:21.862
From an evolutionary perspective, this approach
has been remarkably successful for a dragon

00:02:21.862 --> 00:02:27.120
that, in reality, is a very timid herbivore…

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.170
Venturing deeper into the woodland, I find
that no two dragons have the same survival

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:33.550
strategy.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:38.270
Splashing along the surface of a stream is
a brightly colored Emerald Saeth.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:44.390
Unlike the Nothophthalmus, this more basal
species is covered in fine, wind-resistant

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:45.390
fibers.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:51.220
The prehistoric appearance of Emerald Saeth
sheds light into the origins of microdragons.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:56.760
The distant ancestor of Welsh dragons — the
Dimorphodon Macronyx – branched off from

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:03.160
pterosaurs some 190 million years ago, surviving
the mass extinction that wiped out their more

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:08.140
famous cousins thanks to their diminutive
size and more adaptable nature.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:13.890
Today, modern dragons have branched off into
ever more specialized sub-species, with the

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:17.890
Emerald Saeth a scavenger of fallen seeds
and berries.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:22.500
They also have a fondness for playing among
the spray of rivers, although there appears

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:28.480
to be no practical benefit for this — and
is probably done just for fun…

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.670
Near a patch of wildflowers, a barely audible
buzzing draws me closer.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:39.170
Hovering near a carefully chosen flower is
the fascinating Draco Cepediana.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:45.620
On delicate green and red wings, Draco Cepediana
darts between flowers, feeding on nectar with

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.360
the aid of their long, thin tongue.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:54.420
This behavior closely mirrors that of hummingbirds,
which live a similar high-metabolism lifestyle

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.700
feeding on flower nectar.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.950
It might be surprising that such disparate
species would converge on the same strategy,

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.340
but there’s evidence to support such an
outcome.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:10.430
Hummingbird hawk-moths are pollinating insects
that independently became so similar to real

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:13.860
hummingbirds that the two are often mixed
up.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:19.660
And like all species occupying the hummingbird
niche, the Draco Cepediana performs a vital

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:25.400
role within their local ecosystem — transporting
pollen on the long, red filaments that trail

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:30.220
from their head as they move from flower to
flower.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:35.290
Pollinating plants is one thing, but among
the branches of holly trees, I find one unusual

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:40.480
specialist has taken their connection with
the foliage to new extremes.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.850
Draco Ilexis is a species whose wings and
body have evolved to resemble the leaves of

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:49.870
holly trees — making them appear like living
plants.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:54.480
As strange as this specimen might appear,
there is a long precedent for such camouflage

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.180
in nature.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:59.920
Defensive mimicry is relatively common in
the larger animal kingdom, in particular with

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.290
regards to plants — as mimicking a plant
is a good way to get a carnivore to ignore

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:05.180
you as a food source.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:10.130
If a Draco Ilexis is spotted, however, they
have one last defense.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:14.981
Like the trees they mimic, their wings are
covered in tough, thorn-like spines — which

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:21.930
make Draco Ilexis more trouble than they’re
worth to most predators…

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:27.590
Within the foliage of fruit trees, I discover
another dragon species makes clever use of

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:29.060
plant-mimicry.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:33.490
The rotund Draco Acidium looks like a round
fruit themselves.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:38.360
Although they may appear dangerous with their
protruding fangs, this dragon’s large teeth

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:42.980
are only used to pierce the tough outer peels
of the fruits they feed on.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:49.390
For the Draco Acidium is a frugivore, meaning
their diet consists mainly of raw fruits.

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:54.580
Many animals engage in frugivorous behavior
— and while this might seem like pilfering,

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:58.770
many fruit-producing plants benefit from such
interactions.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:03.710
Certain frugivores actually assist plants
by dispersing their seeds after digesting

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:07.280
them — a type of interaction known as mutualism.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:12.000
Which side of the fence the Draco Acidium
falls on is unknown — but it’s certainly

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.230
possible these tiny dragons act as natural
gardeners of the fruit groves they call home.

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:24.530
Being slow and flightless, Draco Acidium also
rely on these trees to keep them hidden — although

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:30.800
as a last resort, they also possess a powerful,
acidic bite...

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.030
Yet other dragons use camouflage for a more
sinister purpose.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:41.780
Draco Muscipula possesses one of the most
unusual hunting strategies of any dragon.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:47.360
Able to remain absolutely motionless for hours
at a time, they hold their wings over their

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.240
head and release a pungent odor to attract
potential prey.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:57.970
Carnivorous plants like Venus flytraps have
a similar strategy, using sweet smelling nectar

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.900
to lure in unlucky insects.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:06.920
And like a flytrap, once the Draco Muscipula’s
prey is within reach, the dragon claps their

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:11.990
specialized wings together, stunning their
prey before dropping them into the waiting

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:13.650
jaws beneath.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:19.030
For this dragon, patience is more than a virtue…

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:22.360
Yet not all dragons have evolved to blend
in.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:28.730
Although just a few centimeters long, the
vivid coloration of species like Draco Favilla

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:33.040
mean they can be easily spotted — and that’s
exactly the point.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:38.650
Like the glaring colors of the poison dart
frog, the Draco Favilla’s coloration is

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:44.190
aposematic, meaning it warns predators the
species isn’t worth attacking.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:50.120
And while the poison dart frog backs up their
aposematic hue with poison, Draco Favilla’s

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.430
color warns of their deadly venom.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:58.930
Delivered by tiny, sharp-fanged jaws, this
dragon’s venom is potent enough to incapacitate

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:02.020
creatures many times larger than themselves.

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:06.110
I’ll need to watch where I step…

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:10.960
And hidden among the blades of grass, some
dragons are even smaller.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:17.169
Just two centimeters in length, Draco Coccineus
is perhaps the smallest species of dragon

00:08:17.169 --> 00:08:18.540
on the planet.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:23.860
Indeed, they are so small they stretch our
understanding of what a dragon can be.

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:29.010
Yet remarkably, there is some precedent for
a reptile this size.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:34.460
Nano-chameleons are a chameleon subspecies
that might be the smallest reptiles on earth

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:37.880
— at roughly the size of a sunflower seed.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:42.280
At sizes like this, the world can be a daunting
place to navigate.

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:48.260
Luckily, the Draco Coccineus possesses two
hair-like antennae packed with highly sensitive

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:53.100
sense receptors that help them detect potential
threats over long distances.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:09:01.190
And so, this insect-sized dragon does quite
well for themselves within their unusual niche…

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:05.060
In other regions, some dragons grow larger.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:10.350
Around the curve of a woodland stream, I happen
upon a scarcely seen specimen.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:16.770
A Draco Caudatae glistens in the afternoon
sun, body poised for a quick dive into the

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:17.770
water.

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:23.290
The Caudate is an amphibious hunter, with
webbed toes and a rudder-like tail.

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:28.460
The unusually long and narrow snout of this
dragon contains numerous recurved teeth for

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:31.020
latching onto fish.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:36.350
Occupying a niche similar to storks or herons,
the Draco Caudatae are natural fishermen.

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:42.810
They are also experts at stealthy movement,
with their silent nature making them difficult

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:43.810
to track.

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:46.980
I’m lucky to be able to observe one…

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:53.190
Upon the rocky coast, some dragons have become
even more specialized aquatic lifeforms.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:58.450
Perched on a rock in the afternoon sun is
the bizarre Crimson Cimwch.

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:04.810
Possessing a lobster-like tail fluke for paddling
under the frothy waves, this organism fills

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:07.640
a niche one might not expect a dragon to occupy.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:14.510
The Crimson Cimwch hunts in shallow coastal
waters, feeding on mollusks and small fish.

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:19.980
Behaving like seals or sea lions, they spend
a good amount of time out on the water — surveying

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:24.070
their territory, or just lazing about upon
the rocks.

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:29.080
On the front of their snouts, they possess
curious tentacular appendages that further

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:32.880
separate their appearance from the dragons
of fairytales.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:36.870
How such a feature first evolved Is a biological
mystery.

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:42.970
I wonder if I’ll find other species with
a similar adaptation…

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:48.610
Below the surface of the freezing waters,
some specimens have given up on land almost

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:49.830
entirely.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:55.700
The North Nofiwr are sleek, agile aquatic
hunters — using their powerful wings and

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:57.810
broad tail for propulsion.

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:01.890
Their soft, downy feathers keep them warm
in the cold currents.

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:07.800
Feathers on an aquatic animal might seem strange,
but penguins possess feathers that are shorter

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:12.660
and stiffer than those of most birds, keeping
them streamlined in the water.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:17.430
We can guess that the Nofiwr have taken a
page from the penguins’ book when it comes

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:19.270
to their feather structure.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:25.060
Like penguins, the North Nofiwr are ungainly
when not in the water, only able to waddle

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:26.060
about.

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:32.690
In their element, however, these torpedo-like
swimmers are something to behold…

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:37.540
Further up the coast, seabirds flap above
the crashing waves.

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:43.580
And hidden among the flock, fearless Seren
y Storms dive close to the raging tide.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:49.610
These exceptionally rare dragons can be seen
flying high even in the stormiest weather.

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:55.530
With such apparent boldness, it would be easy
to sentimentalize these dragons as courageous.

00:11:55.530 --> 00:12:01.570
Yet like seagulls, Seren y Storms are perhaps
closer to pests than they are to adventurers,

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:06.850
and spend much of their time scavenging the
picnic leftovers of tourists.

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:12.450
While valiantly stealing discarded food, Seren
y Storms often release a call that has been

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:15.390
described as a ‘chuckling sound.’

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:19.660
Considering their surprisingly intelligent
nature, it is tempting to think that these

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:23.670
little nuisances are laughing at us…

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:30.410
Heading inland, I notice that high above the
treetops, a flock soars through the open skies.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:36.580
The pale blue Draco Aerilingii are at first
difficult to spot against the afternoon sky,

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:43.410
but their graceful curved wings and V-shaped
tail give them an unmistakable silhouette.

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:49.250
These graceful creatures are aerial acrobats,
spending much of their time above the canopy,

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:53.870
but able to swoop among the branches in pursuit
of insects.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:59.560
Certain woodland raptors are able to perform
similar feats of agility, pursuing prey at

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:03.680
top speeds through dense forests without hitting
trees.

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:09.250
But the Draco Aerilingii can best display
their swiftness in the open air, where great

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:15.760
flocks such as this one bank and dive for
no reason beyond their personal enjoyment…

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:19.390
Soon, the night will come to the forest.

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:25.110
In the fading light, I find hidden among the
trunks a primeval dragon that has endured

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:26.950
into the modern day.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:32.399
Draco Borealis are a species with a downright
prehistoric appearance.

00:13:32.399 --> 00:13:36.640
Resembling pterosaurs more than many of the
other dragons I’ve encountered, perhaps

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:41.040
the most distinguishing feature of this species
is their colorful throat-pouch.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:46.790
Iguanas have similar structures under their
necks that help with thermoregulation.

00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:52.500
Yet the pouch of Draco Borealis has a far
stranger function — an air reserve.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:58.480
This dragon occasionally dives into lakes
to catch fish, and uses their bizarre pouch

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:01.110
to extend their lung-capacity.

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:07.110
The extremes of dragon survival strategies
cannot be underestimated…

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:12.220
With the sun about to set on the far side
of the valley, I come across a particularly

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:14.700
old region of the woodland.

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:21.350
Here, a heavily-built brute with long curved
horns patrols the rotting branches.

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:25.950
This is Draco Babirusis, and they have come
here for a meal.

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:29.440
But it’s not the sort of meal one might
expect of such a beast.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:35.930
Draco Babirusis is an insectivore, with their
diet composed primarily of ants and termites.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:41.170
They use their highly specialized head to
dig through rotten bark and leaf litter in

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:43.440
search of their crawling food.

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:49.750
Their top horns also serve as defensive structures
that help Draco Babirusis maintain exclusive

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:52.910
rights to their side of the rotting logs.

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:58.790
With the sun now almost gone, I’ll leave
Draco Babirusis to their supper...

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:01.960
In time, dusk falls upon the forest.

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:08.060
In the low light, I find even more alien-looking
dragon species become active.

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:13.339
Lighting up the dark are the glowing eyes
and tails of Draco Lampyridae.

00:15:13.339 --> 00:15:19.390
The function of this natural bioluminescence
isn’t immediately apparent, but we can find

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:22.170
potential clues in the natural world.

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:27.940
The biochemical glow of fireflies is thought
to serve a number of functions — from scaring

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:30.290
off predators to attracting mates.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:36.140
And since these Draco Lampyridae are in a
pair, I wonder if the glow they give off is

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:38.920
perhaps a method of communication.

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:46.230
If this is indeed the case, I don’t want
to interrupt what seems to be a private conversation...

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:51.730
Amongst the ancient and decaying trees of
the darkest, most inaccessible depths of the

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:55.610
forest, a poorly-understood creature is lurking.

00:15:55.610 --> 00:16:01.709
Draco Johansei is scarcely recognizable as
a dragon at a glance — possessing a mass

00:16:01.709 --> 00:16:05.050
of highly dexterous tentacles surrounding
their mouth.

00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:11.800
While I’ve spotted other species with similar
appendages, Draco Johansei takes these appendages

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:13.200
to a new level.

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:18.140
Like the snout of a star-nosed mole, these
appendages help the species navigate their

00:16:18.140 --> 00:16:23.860
environment through tactile input — as the
Draco Johansei are partially sightless.

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:30.480
Yet unlike the star-nosed mole, this dragon
also uses their appendages to ensnare prey.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:35.790
And while details of diet and behavior remain
largely unknown, signs of aggression tell

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:38.850
me to keep my distance...

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:44.670
In the dark woods, I’ve seen dreamlike specimens,
and beasts that seem to have wandered from

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:45.870
a nightmare.

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:49.649
Yet the last dragon I encounter is both at
once.

00:16:49.649 --> 00:16:55.079
Draco Ignisprehendri is an enigmatic omnivore
with a striking appearance.

00:16:55.079 --> 00:17:00.830
They appear to be bioluminescent, with a glow
green liquid dripping from their teeth.

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:07.309
Yet Draco Ignisprehendri’s light is actually
borrowed from their primary food source — glow

00:17:07.309 --> 00:17:08.499
worms.

00:17:08.499 --> 00:17:11.759
These tiny larvae give off a subtle green
glimmer.

00:17:11.759 --> 00:17:17.959
And when mixed with Draco Ignisprehendri’s
saliva, the glimmer becomes a hypnotic light

00:17:17.959 --> 00:17:19.519
in the dark forest.

00:17:19.519 --> 00:17:24.809
Like most species I’ve glimpsed today, Draco
Ignisprehendri doesn’t fit the traditional

00:17:24.809 --> 00:17:26.509
myth of the dragon.

00:17:26.509 --> 00:17:32.500
Yet they are, undoubtedly, a mesmerizing lifeform…

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:37.619
The dragons we’ve seen today may be smaller
than those of myth and legend, but thanks

00:17:37.619 --> 00:17:41.700
to their incredible survival strategies, they
are no less majestic.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:48.220
And there’s so many more species and biological
details to discover in the incredible volumes

00:17:48.220 --> 00:17:49.350
of Andy Frazer.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:55.581
There are even rumors of older dragons that
blend into the moss and stone and that grow

00:17:55.581 --> 00:18:01.760
